import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A1/v0"] = """Problem: In MVFA, only the segmentation adapters produce anomaly maps, and they are trained exclusively with Dice and Focal losses on pixel masks from three BMAD datasets (BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC), while the image-labelled datasets (OCT17, ChestXray, HIS) only ever train the detection adapters. Can the segmentation adapters instead be trained from image-level labels alone — through a multiple-instance objective on each level's segmentation-branch patch-to-text similarity map (primary operator: top-k mean of patch anomaly probabilities, so that an abnormal image must contain a few highly abnormal patches and a normal image none) applied to all five source datasets with masks discarded — and how much of the mask-supervised zero-shot localisation (pixel-level AUROC and PRO) and detection (image-level AUROC) on the held-out modality does this retain? The comparison is mask-supervised MVFA versus image-label-supervised MVFA, both trained and model-selected under the shared protocol.

Rationale: Limitation G3, corrected against the released code, shows that MVFA's localisation depends entirely on dense expert masks from three datasets, and G4 shows that even its few-shot mode needs K abnormal images with masks. Image-level labels are cheap and already available for every BMAD dataset, but MVFA never uses them to teach the segmentation branch where anomalies are. The related literature does not answer whether text-anchored patch similarities in a multi-level CLIP adapter can learn localisation from weak labels: MedSAD-CLIP, MLCA and MadCLIP keep pixel or labelled abnormal supervision, AnoCLIP and SAA+ avoid training altogether, and MedCLIP-SAMv2 uses weak supervision only for a separate segmentation model. The question is narrow — it changes the training signal of one branch — and directly implementable by replacing the seg_loss term in the public training script; a negative result still quantifies what masks are worth for cross-modality localisation. Under the shared protocol, both variants are selected on a source-modality validation split rather than on the target test set, so the comparison is not biased in favour of either."""
A["stage1/problem/B1/v0"] = """Problem: MVFA's few-shot image and pixel scores are produced by min-max normalising the zero-shot and the few-shot branch over the entire test set and adding them with weight 0.5, so the score of one image — and the ranking that defines the reported AUROC — depends on which other images are in the test set. How large is this dependence, and can it be removed by an inductive, per-image fusion that normalises each branch with statistics computed only from the K normal support images (leave-one-out scores of the supports against the memory bank and against the text anchors), together with a robust image-level aggregation (top-k mean instead of the max over the fused map)? Outcomes: (i) the spread of MVFA's fused AUROC across random sub-samples of the test set and under shifted prevalence (10% and 50% abnormal), (ii) image-level and pixel-level AUROC of the inductive fusion versus the transductive original, and (iii) sensitivity at 95% specificity, F1 and expected calibration error at a threshold fixed from the support scores alone, for every held-out BMAD modality and K in {2, 4, 8, 16}.

Rationale: The code verification changed this group of limitations: MVFA does normalise its branches (G8), but with test-set extremes, which makes the method non-deployable on a stream of single images and makes the reported numbers depend on the test set's composition; the image score is a max over the fused map for some datasets and a mean for others (G9, G17), and only AUROC is reported (G11). Related work concentrates on better representations and prompts (MLCA, ClipMPCAD, AnomalyCLIP, HieClip) and evaluates with the same threshold-free AUROC; AnomalyGPT removes thresholds only with a fine-tuned large vision-language model. The K normal support images are a label-free, per-modality reference that MVFA already loads but never uses for normalisation, which makes an inductive fusion possible without new data. The problem is confined to the scoring code (test_few.py), is inference-only on retrained baseline checkpoints, and measures exactly the deployment property — a per-image score with a transferable threshold — that the current evaluation hides."""
A["stage1/problem/D1/v0"] = """Problem: Nothing in MVFA's training objective encodes that normal tissue occupies a compact region of feature space while anomalies are diverse: patches are aligned symmetrically to one 'normal' and one 'abnormal' text anchor, and the few-shot branch memorises raw support patches. Does adding, at each of the four adapter levels, a small set of M learnable normal prototypes trained jointly with the adapters on the source modalities — with a compactness loss that pulls normal patches to their nearest prototype and a margin loss that keeps abnormal patches away — improve zero-shot image-level AUROC, pixel-level AUROC and PRO on the held-out modality, and can the prototypes, re-estimated from the K normal support images, replace the patch memory bank in the few-shot setting without loss of accuracy? M in {8, 32, 128}.

Rationale: Limitations G7 and G13 describe the same missing assumption — compactness of normality — from the architecture and the memory side, and G5 notes that the single static text notion of 'normal' is shared across all modalities. Related work addresses asymmetry differently: MadCLIP uses dual normal/abnormal adapter branches with a SigLIP loss, APRIL-GAN and MVFA keep per-level memory banks, and prototype methods in universal anomaly detection fit prototypes from one image or post hoc. Learning prototypes jointly with the adapters on multiple source modalities, and testing whether they transfer to an unseen modality, is a contained change to the training objective of the public code and is feasible on one GPU; the shared protocol prevents prototype hyper-parameters from being tuned on the target test set."""
